# Case: \c and \C in a pattern, with 'ignorecase' at its default (off).

=== a pattern matches case by default
ref:    pattern.txt */\c*
quote:  foo	  off		-		foo
given:  |Foo foo FOO
keys:   :s/foo/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |Foo (foo) FOO

=== \c at the start ignores case
ref:    pattern.txt */\c*
quote:  \cfoo	  -		-		foo Foo FOO
given:  |Foo foo FOO
keys:   :s/\cfoo/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(Foo) (foo) (FOO)

=== \c anywhere in the pattern applies to all of it
ref:    pattern.txt */\c*
quote:  When "\c" appears anywhere in the pattern, the whole pattern is handled like 'ignorecase' is on.
given:  |Foo FOO
keys:   :s/fo\co/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(Foo) (FOO)

=== \C forces matching case
ref:    pattern.txt */\c*
quote:  foo\C	  -		-		foo
given:  |Foo foo
keys:   :s/foo\C/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |Foo (foo)

=== \c with a collection
ref:    pattern.txt */[]*
quote:  \c[a-z]$	same
given:  |ab cD
keys:   :s/\c[a-z]$/(&)/<CR>
expect: |ab c(D)

=== \c ignores case of a backreference
ref:    pattern.txt */\c*
quote:  When "\c" appears anywhere in the pattern, the whole pattern is handled like 'ignorecase' is on.
given:  |aA bc
keys:   :s/\c\(a\)\1/(&)/<CR>
expect: |(aA) bc

=== \c ignores case of multibyte letters
ref:    pattern.txt */\c*
quote:  When "\c" appears anywhere in the pattern, the whole pattern is handled like 'ignorecase' is on.
given:  |xÉé
keys:   :s/\cé/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |x(É)(é)
